\fs32\b EXTRACTED - TRYHACKME FORENSIC WALKTHROUGH\b0\par
\fs22 Public portfolio edition\par
\par
\b Executive Summary\b0\par
PCAP-driven investigation covering HTTP PowerShell delivery, TCP stream recovery, Base64/XOR reversal, KeePass process-dump analysis, candidate credential validation, and KeePass vault inspection.\par
\par
\b Investigation Chain\b0\par
PCAP -> HTTP PowerShell -> TCP/1337 + TCP/1338 -> Base64 -> XOR -> .dmp + .kdbx -> memory analysis -> validation\par
\par
\b 1. Evidence and Triage\b0\par
The capture reveals an HTTP-delivered PowerShell script. The payload identifies two exfiltration paths: TCP/1337 for a process dump and TCP/1338 for a KeePass database.\par
\par
\b 2. Recovery\b0\par
Reverse Base64 first, then apply the correct single-byte XOR key. Validate the outputs with file and strings.\par
\par
\b 3. Memory Forensics\b0\par
A KeePass process dump may contain runtime credential material. Dedicated dump-analysis tooling can recover candidate key material; unknown characters should be validated against the recovered database rather than trusted blindly.\par
\par
\b 4. Vault Validation\b0\par
Use keepass2john/John the Ripper to verify candidates and KeePassXC CLI to confirm access to the target entry.\par
\par
\b Public Safety\b0\par
The original flag, exact master password, and unique vault UUID are intentionally redacted.\par
\par
